%% System design
%% The existing quiet vendor mount owns a parsed snapshot, owner, history, and five-row offset.
%% History is all, created_at, or last_contact. Owner and history intersect before slicing in server order.
%% The alternative of separate history lists duplicates ordering and paging. One projection keeps one snapshot.
%% Current capacity is the server's 1000-row cap. At 10x, linear filtering remains local but the server cap refuses overflow first.
%% Load spread, storage access, N+1, queues, circuit breakers, and rate limits are unchanged: controls make no external calls.
%% One existing read on opening or refresh replaces the snapshot. There is no persistent cache or invalidation service.
%% Failure refuses current rows; asynchronous reads cannot publish after disposal. Only absent owners reset on refresh.
%% Scaling needs no infrastructure. Consistency comes from one validated snapshot. No writes or sends need idempotency keys.
stateDiagram-v2
  [*] --> Collapsed
  Collapsed --> Loading: Show for the first time
  Loading --> Visible: Valid snapshot arrives
  Loading --> Refused: Read fails or parser refuses
  Refused --> Loading: Refresh
  Visible --> Visible: Choose history or owner, reset to first page
  Visible --> Visible: Next or previous page
  Visible --> Collapsed: Hide, retain choices
  Collapsed --> Visible: Show loaded snapshot, retain choices
  Visible --> Loading: Refresh, retain history
  Visible --> Record: Open review link
  Record --> Visible: Close review, return focus
  Visible --> Retired: Session ends or mount disposed
  Loading --> Retired: Dispose before read settles
  Retired --> [*]
%% never infer history from dates or display values
%% never read on filter or page changes
%% never show old rows after refusal or retirement
